CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: integration-only
SCALE_REALISM: L0 does not establish realistic workload behavior. Evidence is a CPU stdlib simulation with 40 sessions x 8 turns, synthetic 1:1 committed/transient scratch, no GPU allocator behavior, no continuous batching/scheduler interaction, no TTFT, and no real vLLM/SGLang/TRT-LLM cache implementation. A GPU pass on vLLM would test implementation cost, but vLLM is not the strongest prior-art baseline because TensorRT-LLM already exposes the needed token-range priority mechanism.
RATIONALE: Prompt version v001. The claimed mechanism is "evict TRANSIENT tool payloads first while preserving COMMITTED conversation scratch and a hot-prefix floor." TensorRT-LLM's shipping API already supports priority/duration for discrete token ranges and explicitly gives the two relevant examples: keep system prompt blocks high priority and set blocks that will never be reused to lowest priority so they are evicted first. That is not just adjacent reuse infrastructure; it is the same retention control surface with the semantic labels supplied by the application. SGLang/RadixAttention and AttentionStore further reduce the systems content of the claim by covering prefix reuse and multi-turn committed-history reuse. The packet's L0 numbers support that this policy can beat LRU/pin in a toy trace, but they do not distinguish a systems design contribution from applying existing priority-retention knobs to agent tool-token ranges.
